WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.044
- I'm not really content creatively,

00:00:03.044 --> 00:00:05.160
unless I'm doing something that

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.138
scares me a little bit.

00:00:06.138 --> 00:00:09.395
It's just the more you
know about what's possible

00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:11.674
and how to do it, the
more freedom you have.

00:00:11.674 --> 00:00:13.791
Whatever, if it makes you laugh,

00:00:13.791 --> 00:00:15.136
it's an honest laugh.

00:00:15.136 --> 00:00:16.802
We're going through a bit of a dark ages

00:00:16.802 --> 00:00:18.835
as far as FCC restrictions and

00:00:18.835 --> 00:00:20.426
it's a lot that we can't do now,

00:00:20.426 --> 00:00:22.947
that we used to be able to
do when the show started.

00:00:22.947 --> 00:00:24.654
- He's an American TV producer,

00:00:24.654 --> 00:00:26.932
filmmaker, actor, and singer.

00:00:26.932 --> 00:00:28.509
He's best known as the creator

00:00:28.509 --> 00:00:30.465
of the TV series, Family Guy.

00:00:30.465 --> 00:00:32.415
His first feature length film, Ted,

00:00:32.415 --> 00:00:35.792
became the highest grossing
original R-rated comedy.

00:00:35.792 --> 00:00:37.704
He's Seth MacFarlane and here's my take on

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.449
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:41.150
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.852
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:42.852 --> 00:00:44.238
for a special bonus clip.

00:00:44.238 --> 00:00:45.621
And as always as Seth is talking,

00:00:45.621 --> 00:00:47.740
if he says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.254
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:49.254 --> 00:00:51.049
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:51.049 --> 00:00:52.714
can be inspired as well.

00:00:52.714 --> 00:00:53.547
Enjoy.

00:00:56.581 --> 00:01:00.248
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.507
- What were you like as a child?

00:01:08.507 --> 00:01:12.424
- I was a combination of
reclusive and obsessed

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:15.625
with what I wanted to do.

00:01:15.625 --> 00:01:18.679
When I was two, I was
sitting in front of the TV

00:01:18.679 --> 00:01:21.124
drawing Fred Flintstones
and Woody Woodpeckers,

00:01:21.124 --> 00:01:22.958
and at age seven, I'm running around

00:01:22.958 --> 00:01:25.199
like a crazy person doing voices.

00:01:25.199 --> 00:01:26.907
But at the same time I would try to make

00:01:26.907 --> 00:01:28.983
animated films and try to figure out

00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:32.267
exactly how that art form worked.

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:33.932
- When did you do your first cartoon?

00:01:33.932 --> 00:01:35.517
How old were you and what was it?

00:01:35.517 --> 00:01:37.105
- I was in seventh grade.

00:01:37.105 --> 00:01:38.327
It was called Space Pirates.

00:01:38.327 --> 00:01:39.303
It was terrible.

00:01:39.303 --> 00:01:40.826
I like your bustle by the way.

00:01:40.826 --> 00:01:43.309
- Oh yeah, I really love
that the most alluring

00:01:43.309 --> 00:01:45.668
fashion statement a woman can make today

00:01:45.668 --> 00:01:47.717
is to simulate a fat (bleep).

00:01:47.717 --> 00:01:50.206
- Is the simulation of a
fat (bleep) right there?

00:01:50.206 --> 00:01:52.520
- We don't often see you
in front of the camera.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.980
So what made you think this
time, alright, here we go?

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.607
- Throughout the course of my career,

00:01:57.607 --> 00:02:00.225
I've managed to continue
to challenge myself

00:02:00.225 --> 00:02:04.043
and to try things that I haven't tried

00:02:04.043 --> 00:02:06.041
and to keep scaring myself.

00:02:06.041 --> 00:02:08.850
I'm not really content creatively,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.066
unless I'm doing something
that scares me a little bit.

00:02:12.066 --> 00:02:12.899
That's stimulating.

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:14.224
- There's something inside you that keeps

00:02:14.224 --> 00:02:17.352
saying, no go further,
try something different.

00:02:17.352 --> 00:02:18.185
What is that?

00:02:18.185 --> 00:02:20.900
- It's a constant crippling
feeling of inadequacy.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:22.324
(laughing)

00:02:22.324 --> 00:02:23.424
- Is that what it is?

00:02:23.424 --> 00:02:24.890
- I think that might be what it is, yeah.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:27.494
- We spoke to your dad at
the premiere last night.

00:02:27.494 --> 00:02:28.428
And here's what he said.

00:02:28.428 --> 00:02:31.033
- Just have such great respect for him.

00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:34.033
And he's still a very, very grounded

00:02:35.659 --> 00:02:38.343
and kind and focused human being.

00:02:38.343 --> 00:02:40.500
He's a good soul, a good person

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.251
and that's more than anything else,

00:02:42.251 --> 00:02:43.596
what I'm proud of.

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:45.423
- My dad's such a (bleep).

00:02:45.423 --> 00:02:47.673
(laughing)

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:50.586
I'm going to get him for that, man.

00:02:50.586 --> 00:02:54.201
Oh man, I'm going to,
yeah son of a (bleep).

00:02:54.201 --> 00:02:56.885
From start to finish it
takes about nine months

00:02:56.885 --> 00:02:58.472
to, sometimes longer,

00:02:58.472 --> 00:03:00.261
sometimes 10 months.

00:03:00.261 --> 00:03:02.634
It's roughly a year between the time

00:03:02.634 --> 00:03:05.935
that the story is pitched to
the time it gets on the air.

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:07.603
Takes roughly a year to do it.

00:03:07.603 --> 00:03:10.249
And it's, you know, no
one has ever figured out

00:03:10.249 --> 00:03:14.106
a quicker way to do hand
drawn animation in prime time.

00:03:14.106 --> 00:03:15.569
And it's all still hand drawn.

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:16.875
It's colored with computers

00:03:16.875 --> 00:03:19.687
but every single frame
is still done by hand.

00:03:19.687 --> 00:03:22.258
You know, this season was a
little smoother than last season

00:03:22.258 --> 00:03:24.376
and last season was a little
smoother than the one before.

00:03:24.376 --> 00:03:26.083
So, it gets incrementally easier.

00:03:26.083 --> 00:03:28.726
It's still in many ways,
a seven day a week job

00:03:28.726 --> 00:03:29.781
to get this thing done.

00:03:29.781 --> 00:03:33.040
I mean, it's a virtually
impossible thing to do

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.405
a primetime animated series, yet somehow

00:03:35.405 --> 00:03:38.488
there's a bunch of people that do it.

00:03:42.614 --> 00:03:44.772
It's, I don't know how
The Simpsons, you know,

00:03:44.772 --> 00:03:46.607
does it for as long as
they've been doing it

00:03:46.607 --> 00:03:50.107
but it's just a very, very intense process

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:55.560
that in many ways gets easier
when you get cancelled.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.799
- What advice can you
give to someone who wants

00:03:57.799 --> 00:03:59.141
to study animation?

00:03:59.141 --> 00:04:00.365
- There's a million ways to do it.

00:04:00.365 --> 00:04:02.042
It's trial and error.

00:04:02.042 --> 00:04:04.166
With me it was just trial and error.

00:04:04.166 --> 00:04:06.416
(stammers)

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.701
It is learning the process.

00:04:09.701 --> 00:04:11.447
You do have to find a good school.

00:04:11.447 --> 00:04:12.745
I mean that's the best thing you could do.

00:04:12.745 --> 00:04:14.779
Rhode Island School of Design was great.

00:04:14.779 --> 00:04:16.001
Cal Arts is great.

00:04:16.001 --> 00:04:20.723
I mean there's a lot of
art schools that now have

00:04:20.723 --> 00:04:25.321
gotten up-to-date with
their animation programs.

00:04:25.321 --> 00:04:27.197
But it's just doing it.

00:04:27.197 --> 00:04:29.109
It's doing as much of it as you can

00:04:29.109 --> 00:04:32.004
and sitting, you know,
even if it's with one of

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:33.919
those little cheap video camera things

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:35.833
just experimenting with movement

00:04:35.833 --> 00:04:38.891
and experimenting with speeds of movement

00:04:38.891 --> 00:04:41.047
and what looks funny.

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:43.245
It's just the more you know about

00:04:43.245 --> 00:04:45.359
what's possible and how to do it,

00:04:45.359 --> 00:04:47.398
the more freedom you have.

00:04:47.398 --> 00:04:49.998
It's the same as directing, I guess.

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:51.463
The same as directing live action.

00:04:51.463 --> 00:04:52.519
- You just got to go out and do it.

00:04:52.519 --> 00:04:55.777
- Yeah. If you know what is possible and

00:04:55.777 --> 00:04:58.096
what you're capable of, then

00:04:58.096 --> 00:04:59.519
- Learn the tools first.

00:04:59.519 --> 00:05:00.352
- Yeah.

00:05:00.352 --> 00:05:01.185
Yeah.

00:05:01.185 --> 00:05:02.018
- And how they work.

00:05:02.018 --> 00:05:02.851
- Yeah.

00:05:02.851 --> 00:05:04.680
- [Barbara Walters] And
at 24, he became the

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.156
youngest person to ever
head a network show.

00:05:07.156 --> 00:05:10.418
That led to the spin-offs,
The Cleveland Show

00:05:10.418 --> 00:05:13.434
- Here. Here's 10 bucks.
Don't get kidnapped.

00:05:13.434 --> 00:05:15.143
- and American Dad.

00:05:15.143 --> 00:05:16.654
- Stop get off me.

00:05:16.654 --> 00:05:19.747
- Yeah that's what your
mom said last night.

00:05:19.747 --> 00:05:23.094
- There doesn't seem to
be anything off limits.

00:05:23.094 --> 00:05:26.228
I made a little list of some
of the things that you discuss.

00:05:26.228 --> 00:05:27.145
Bestiality,

00:05:28.019 --> 00:05:29.526
abortion,

00:05:29.526 --> 00:05:30.628
AIDS,

00:05:30.628 --> 00:05:32.795
Irish-men who drink, nuns,

00:05:33.849 --> 00:05:36.170
pre-mature ejaculation,

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:38.161
breastfeeding, and farts.

00:05:38.161 --> 00:05:38.994
- Yeah.

00:05:38.994 --> 00:05:40.560
- You think it's easy
for me to say all that?

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:41.944
- When you line it all up like that,

00:05:41.944 --> 00:05:43.690
I mean it sounds like maybe
we made a huge mistake.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:45.033
Doesn't it?

00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:46.944
- You know what I am back at the ranch?

00:05:46.944 --> 00:05:48.735
I'm a breeding bull.

00:05:48.735 --> 00:05:50.487
- Well, well what, what's that?

00:05:50.487 --> 00:05:52.113
- You gon' find out.

00:05:52.113 --> 00:05:53.888
- [Barbara Walters] The
trick to getting it all

00:05:53.888 --> 00:05:55.898
past the censors, according to Seth,

00:05:55.898 --> 00:06:00.500
is not to offend to many
groups in any given episode.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:02.332
- It all just can't be in the same place.

00:06:02.332 --> 00:06:04.205
There's something called tonnage.

00:06:04.205 --> 00:06:06.705
And we try to keep those jokes

00:06:09.882 --> 00:06:10.978
spread apart.

00:06:10.978 --> 00:06:13.132
It is a balance but my view is that

00:06:13.132 --> 00:06:15.209
whatever, if it makes you laugh,

00:06:15.209 --> 00:06:17.000
it's an honest laugh.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.224
- [Interviewer] You
push the envelope a lot.

00:06:18.224 --> 00:06:19.057
- Yes!

00:06:19.057 --> 00:06:21.838
- [Interviewer] Obviously, you do.

00:06:21.838 --> 00:06:23.956
Has there ever been something that's like,

00:06:23.956 --> 00:06:25.450
oh God, that's a little too,

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:26.535
a little tense even for us,

00:06:26.535 --> 00:06:28.329
so we can't do that?

00:06:28.329 --> 00:06:30.912
- Yeah, there's the occasional,

00:06:33.173 --> 00:06:34.719
joke about,

00:06:34.719 --> 00:06:39.112
that's too racist or too,
you know, offensive to

00:06:39.112 --> 00:06:41.918
people with a particular
disease or, you know,

00:06:41.918 --> 00:06:45.335
there's too much abortion centric comedy.

00:06:46.939 --> 00:06:49.378
That we say, probably that shouldn't go.

00:06:49.378 --> 00:06:52.105
Those are just three
examples that come to mind.

00:06:52.105 --> 00:06:55.608
Probably that maybe
shouldn't go in the show.

00:06:55.608 --> 00:06:57.241
Maybe just for the DVD.

00:06:57.241 --> 00:06:59.678
But there are things that we do now

00:06:59.678 --> 00:07:01.999
that we do strictly for, you know we do

00:07:01.999 --> 00:07:03.421
a few versions of the show.

00:07:03.421 --> 00:07:05.215
There's one for television and

00:07:05.215 --> 00:07:09.298
then there's one for the
DVD because it's a very,

00:07:11.156 --> 00:07:12.827
We're going through a bit of a dark ages

00:07:12.827 --> 00:07:14.743
as far as FCC restrictions.

00:07:14.743 --> 00:07:16.530
There's a lot we can't do now that

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.584
we used to be able to do
when the show started.

00:07:19.584 --> 00:07:23.489
And so, to kind of fight
that, we do DVD versions

00:07:23.489 --> 00:07:27.486
in which you can kind of
see the show untouched.

00:07:27.486 --> 00:07:28.748
Okay, here's one.

00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:30.712
If you were gay and you had to have sex

00:07:30.712 --> 00:07:33.360
with either John Forsythe or Sean Connery,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.693
who would it be?

00:07:35.963 --> 00:07:38.198
Eh, it's so funny I would
also say John Forsythe.

00:07:38.198 --> 00:07:39.501
I was just curious.

00:07:39.501 --> 00:07:42.201
You know, there are great
things about television

00:07:42.201 --> 00:07:44.356
and there are great things about film.

00:07:44.356 --> 00:07:47.329
Television has the advantage of speed.

00:07:47.329 --> 00:07:51.496
You can conceive an idea and
see it through to fruition

00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:54.618
all in the timeframe
that you're just getting

00:07:54.618 --> 00:07:55.923
a movie off the ground.

00:07:55.923 --> 00:08:00.445
You can tell different
types of stories every week

00:08:00.445 --> 00:08:02.195
and if one of 'em is,

00:08:03.702 --> 00:08:05.208
you know you can experiment a little more.

00:08:05.208 --> 00:08:06.558
If one of them goes wrong,

00:08:06.558 --> 00:08:07.699
well there's always next week.

00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:10.749
With a movie, the flip side of that

00:08:10.749 --> 00:08:12.297
is the one story that you tell,

00:08:12.297 --> 00:08:13.681
you can really get it right.

00:08:13.681 --> 00:08:14.514
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:08:14.514 --> 00:08:15.888
So, it depends on the project.

00:08:15.888 --> 00:08:18.290
Generally, the idea comes first.

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:21.787
If I have an idea, then I
sort of think to myself,

00:08:21.787 --> 00:08:24.353
well is this better as a
series or as a feature?

00:08:24.353 --> 00:08:27.644
And I think that's, I think
that's a good way to do it.

00:08:27.644 --> 00:08:29.843
- You're slaving away six months.

00:08:29.843 --> 00:08:32.160
Slaving away because you're
actually pursuing a dream

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:34.563
and it's incredibly hard work.

00:08:34.563 --> 00:08:36.882
But it also, I'm guessing
kind of feels like

00:08:36.882 --> 00:08:38.309
Jesus, this is kind of it.

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:39.777
And I've really got to,

00:08:39.777 --> 00:08:41.975
I do have to pour
everything I have into this.

00:08:41.975 --> 00:08:45.226
- Yeah, they had given me, you know,

00:08:45.226 --> 00:08:47.429
You're talking about The Life of Larry or

00:08:47.429 --> 00:08:48.860
are you talking about the?

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.029
- [Interviewer] Well, the pilot for Fox.

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:51.341
- For Fox, yeah.

00:08:51.341 --> 00:08:53.711
Yeah that, they gave me
something like $40,000

00:08:53.711 --> 00:08:55.542
to do this animated pilot.

00:08:55.542 --> 00:09:00.181
Usually, it's about anywhere
from 800,000 to a million

00:09:00.181 --> 00:09:01.444
to do an animated pilot.

00:09:01.444 --> 00:09:04.111
And I just said yes, I can do it

00:09:05.188 --> 00:09:07.709
and I'll figure out how
to do it after the fact.

00:09:07.709 --> 00:09:08.764
- [Interviewer] And you did.

00:09:08.764 --> 00:09:09.957
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:09.957 --> 00:09:12.030
And it's the kind of thing where

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:15.771
it was a once and a lifetime opportunity

00:09:15.771 --> 00:09:17.847
where they're basically saying, you know,

00:09:17.847 --> 00:09:19.927
we'll give you a small amount of money,

00:09:19.927 --> 00:09:21.841
you're basically going
to have to kill yourself

00:09:21.841 --> 00:09:22.817
for half a year,

00:09:22.817 --> 00:09:24.150
but if it works,

00:09:25.177 --> 00:09:26.510
then you're set.

00:09:27.408 --> 00:09:29.690
And, you know, you've gotten in.

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:30.523
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:09:30.523 --> 00:09:34.370
- So it was a gamble, but I managed to,

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:35.427
managed to do it.

00:09:35.427 --> 00:09:37.099
- [Interviewer] Do you try to stay timely?

00:09:37.099 --> 00:09:38.526
The Paris Hilton thing
today, would you ever

00:09:38.526 --> 00:09:40.193
move into that area?

00:09:42.629 --> 00:09:43.805
- Yeah, you know it's possible.

00:09:43.805 --> 00:09:47.972
It's tough with things that
are very, very immediate

00:09:48.904 --> 00:09:51.552
because of the fact that
it does take us a year

00:09:51.552 --> 00:09:52.854
to do these things.

00:09:52.854 --> 00:09:53.909
You know, if we do,

00:09:53.909 --> 00:09:56.228
We did a joke about when Ashlee Simpson

00:09:56.228 --> 00:09:58.670
had the lip-syncing business
on Saturday Night Live.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:01.353
We did a joke on that and we were--

00:10:01.353 --> 00:10:02.876
By the time it aired, we were just

00:10:02.876 --> 00:10:05.398
on the tail end of relevance because

00:10:05.398 --> 00:10:08.128
it had already, many months had passed.

00:10:08.128 --> 00:10:11.529
And, you know, I think
that's a good thing, though.

00:10:11.529 --> 00:10:13.199
Because I think it's a
good thing for longevity.

00:10:13.199 --> 00:10:14.788
I think it's a good thing for re-runs.

00:10:14.788 --> 00:10:16.294
If you look at, excuse me,

00:10:16.294 --> 00:10:18.264
If you look at All in the Family,

00:10:18.264 --> 00:10:20.381
you know that's a show
that was very, very topical

00:10:20.381 --> 00:10:22.092
and very much of its time,

00:10:22.092 --> 00:10:24.527
but it was general enough that

00:10:24.527 --> 00:10:26.939
you can watch it now and not have to know

00:10:26.939 --> 00:10:29.860
what was going on that specific
week with Richard Nixon

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:31.889
and you could still enjoy it.

00:10:31.889 --> 00:10:36.002
And that's sort of the balance
that we like to go for.

00:10:36.002 --> 00:10:38.255
Alright, guys the best thing
you could do for your body

00:10:38.255 --> 00:10:42.280
after a massage, is hot
coffee, alcohol, and Slim Jims.

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:43.992
- You could be an impressionist.

00:10:43.992 --> 00:10:44.825
(laughing)

00:10:44.825 --> 00:10:46.628
If you wanted to make your living

00:10:46.628 --> 00:10:47.772
as a you know--

00:10:47.772 --> 00:10:48.910
- Fred Travelena.

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:51.921
- There are many times
at a table read that we

00:10:51.921 --> 00:10:54.521
write a piece for somebody
and want to get a guest star

00:10:54.521 --> 00:10:56.026
and Seth will fill in and do it

00:10:56.026 --> 00:10:57.733
or Seth Green will, or I will

00:10:57.733 --> 00:11:00.780
and nine times out of
10, MacFarlane's read

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:03.269
for whoever is way funnier than when

00:11:03.269 --> 00:11:05.062
we actually book the person to do it.

00:11:05.062 --> 00:11:06.530
(audience laughing)

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:08.811
And many times he'll end
up re-recording anyway.

00:11:08.811 --> 00:11:11.377
- Who else can he do that we
would be very amused to hear

00:11:11.377 --> 00:11:13.167
(laughing)

00:11:13.167 --> 00:11:14.305
- Who?

00:11:14.305 --> 00:11:15.806
- Kermit the Frog.

00:11:15.806 --> 00:11:17.312
(audience laughing)

00:11:17.312 --> 00:11:18.446
- Kermit the Frog.

00:11:18.446 --> 00:11:19.747
- Do that Taken thing?

00:11:19.747 --> 00:11:21.702
We have a gag in the show coming with

00:11:21.702 --> 00:11:23.243
Jim Henson's, Taken.

00:11:23.243 --> 00:11:24.076
- Yeah.

00:11:24.076 --> 00:11:26.659
(all laughing)

00:11:28.702 --> 00:11:31.379
- I don't have any money.

00:11:31.379 --> 00:11:32.330
(audience laughing)

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:34.729
But what I do have are
a certain set of skills.

00:11:34.729 --> 00:11:37.312
(all laughing)

00:11:42.465 --> 00:11:44.910
Skills that could be a
nightmare for someone like you.

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:47.493
(all laughing)

00:11:49.068 --> 00:11:50.615
If you don't let the girl go,

00:11:50.615 --> 00:11:52.529
(laughing)

00:11:52.529 --> 00:11:55.498
I will track you down, I will find you

00:11:55.498 --> 00:11:57.058
and I will kill you.

00:11:57.058 --> 00:11:58.846
(audience clapping)

00:11:58.846 --> 00:12:00.763
That's Kermit the Frog.

00:12:04.838 --> 00:12:06.302
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:12:06.302 --> 00:12:09.197
I made this video because
Gerardo asked me to.

00:12:09.197 --> 00:12:10.422
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:12:10.422 --> 00:12:12.216
that you want me to profile next,

00:12:12.216 --> 00:12:13.278
leave it in the comments below

00:12:13.278 --> 00:12:14.418
and I'll see what I can do.

00:12:14.418 --> 00:12:16.125
I'd also like to know what did Seth say

00:12:16.125 --> 00:12:17.633
that really resonated with you?

00:12:17.633 --> 00:12:18.812
Leave it in the comments.

00:12:18.812 --> 00:12:19.992
Really curious to find out.

00:12:19.992 --> 00:12:21.294
Finally, want to give a quick shout out to

00:12:21.294 --> 00:12:23.247
Shahrukh, thank you so much for picking up

00:12:23.247 --> 00:12:25.728
a copy of my book and
posting that tweet about it.

00:12:25.728 --> 00:12:27.440
I really, really, really appreciate it and

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:28.419
I hope you enjoy.

00:12:28.419 --> 00:12:30.087
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:12:30.087 --> 00:12:30.940
I believe in you.

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:33.055
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:12:33.055 --> 00:12:36.692
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:12:36.692 --> 00:12:38.192
I'll see you soon.

00:12:43.651 --> 00:12:45.449
- Alright, we have got work to do.

00:12:45.449 --> 00:12:47.691
So, let's roll up our
sleeves and get right to it.

00:12:47.691 --> 00:12:48.524
There's a lot to do.

00:12:48.524 --> 00:12:50.379
So we can't be afraid to
just roll up our sleeves

00:12:50.379 --> 00:12:51.477
and get the job done.

00:12:51.477 --> 00:12:52.859
We can't be hands off managers.

00:12:52.859 --> 00:12:54.811
We got to roll up our
sleeves and just dig in.

00:12:54.811 --> 00:12:56.759
- Peter your arms are turning purple.

00:12:56.759 --> 00:12:58.104
- Purple with success.

00:12:58.104 --> 00:12:59.412
That's the color of money.

00:12:59.412 --> 00:13:01.649
You got to do the work of two
men and pay yourself for one.

00:13:01.649 --> 00:13:04.211
Just roll up those sleeves
and address the task at hand.

00:13:04.211 --> 00:13:05.273
Whatever it may be.

00:13:05.273 --> 00:13:06.817
Not shying away from the dirty work

00:13:06.817 --> 00:13:08.689
but just rolling up our
sleeves and having at it.

00:13:08.689 --> 00:13:10.605
Success is not given, it is taken.

00:13:10.605 --> 00:13:13.256
It's time to get in
the game and get dirty.

00:13:13.256 --> 00:13:15.589
Alright, I'm going to lunch.

